Use P001/V001/ingest 2 and O008-C1, objective revision 1. Supported instruction can use this block and linked notes. Independent acquisition and delayed attempts are closed note, with blank paper and pen only; no calculator, device, AI, hint or key exposure. Present only the question before the learner responds; keys remain teacher-side. Unknown help/access is unscorable for independence. Hypothetical inputs instantiate supported mechanisms and are not empirical findings.

## Prerequisites and checks

No prior course objective is required; the focused foundational diagnostic below establishes only entry conditions.

No upstream curriculum objective required. O008-P1 v1 source-reading check: “A document lists an exam date, but a learner has not chosen an attempt. Does the list itself establish that learner’s personal deadline?” Key: no; an offered slot differs from a chosen deadline. Pass requires the distinction; partial if no without explanation; fail if the deadline is assumed. Repair Explanation steps 1 and 4. This check never grants acquisition.

Entry checks have item version v1, and pass/partial/fail plus repair routes above. Their exact limited outcomes do not certify the upstream objective. A waiver requires actual committed compatible evidence, never familiarity or assumed mastery. If a diagnostic fails, use its named prepared explanation and support before continuing; do not cycle through full upstream independent items just to diagnose.

## Teaching order and rationale

1. Retrieve [[03_Agents/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Behavioral_Economics/lessons/O008.md#Motivation, intuition, and definitions]] and ask why the comparison matters. Introduce notation and boundaries before calculation.
2. Use [[03_Agents/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Behavioral_Economics/lessons/O008.md#Explanation steps]] in numbered order; each step supplies a dependency for the next.
3. Show [[03_Agents/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Behavioral_Economics/lessons/O008.md#Worked example]] once, exposing reasoning and checks.
4. Ask O008-S1 in [[03_Agents/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Behavioral_Economics/lessons/O008.md#Practice with fading assistance]]; let the learner fill missing steps, then reduce assistance only after an actual adequate response.
5. Ask unused O008-I1 then O008-I2 separately. Score before feedback; any help makes that attempt supported.
6. If the response shows a gap, use [[03_Agents/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Behavioral_Economics/lessons/O008.md#Misconceptions and correction]] and fresh C1 as repair. Return to an unused full acquisition item only if available; otherwise escalate.
7. After qualifying acquisition, recall may schedule a real interval; teach starts an unused D1 or D2 before any recap and checks elapsed time/exposure.

Known prerequisite evidence skips only its diagnostic/support. Full current-objective checks remain required. Two parallel numeric cases demonstrate this bounded outcome, not generalized mastery of untaught models.

## Motivation, intuition, and definitions

Course information describes the uploaded documents. Its purpose is to prevent preparation under incompatible exam conditions and to keep the boundary of available content visible. A listed date is an option rather than the learner’s chosen deadline; document facts do not prove current availability or full exam coverage.

Visual support:

No visual asset is necessary for this objective; use the explicit equations, labels and reasoning prepared here.

## Explanation steps

1. Identify the course and its conventional prerequisite, then reproduce the theory–evidence–application study sequence and supplied appointment information. The documents describe schedules, without a live availability check.
2. Distinguish assessment format from preparation tools. Supported practice can use notes, but independent checks mirror the supplied closed-book restriction with only blank paper and pens. Exactly one correct multiple-choice option differs from multiple-response selection; open text requires reasoning. No negative points does not invent a partial-credit policy. Refusing the integrity declaration prevents evaluation and is F.
3. Read the dates as offered slots. The exam lasts 80 minutes, maximum 100 points, at most three attempts despite four slots; registration and deregistration have separate 48/24-hour limits. Printed grade ranges leave finer fractions and rounding unspecified. Do not fill those gaps or schedule an attempt.
4. Separate the original introduction from the expanded social-preferences collection. Later syllabus/sample topics identify future scope; they do not supply an answer key, formal later models or discrimination/intergroup-contact readings. Current social-preference concepts are supported without making every sample question ready. Misperceptions has a SIS condition absent from the syllabus. No exported poll responses exist.
5. This first plan has an unknown personal deadline (null) and partial whole-semester coverage. Any chosen exam/timing constraint needs explicit user evidence; later academic material goes to ingest before curriculum expansion.

## Worked example

### O008-W1 — Reading an orientation record

Item version: v1. Role: worked instruction.

**Setup:** A hypothetical learner reads four exam slots and proposes four attempts, a calculator and treating the example exam as a complete keyed syllabus. Reconstruct the full administrative facts and content boundary.

**Worked reasoning and result:** The supplied documents identify Behavioral Economics JEB147, taught in person by Michal Bauer and Julie Chytilová, with Microeconomics I JEB104 as prerequisite. Preparation combines theory/intuition, experimental evidence and applications; slides are posted in SIS and papers are strongly encouraged. Bauer’s syllabus office is 402 with Friday 11:00–11:45; Chytilová’s is 410 with Friday 10:30–11:45. Email first; the introduction also says Bauer appointments can be arranged by email. These are supplied descriptions, not live availability. The grade is based on the written final: maximum 100 points, 80 minutes, one-correct-answer multiple choice and open-ended text, no negative points for incorrect answers. It is closed book: supporting materials, devices, calculators and AI tools prohibited; blank paper and pens allowed. Integrity-declaration agreement is required for evaluation; refusal is F. Listed exam slots are Dec 16, 2026 08:00–09:20; Jan 15, 2027 09:30–10:50; Jan 29, 2027 09:30–10:50; Feb 5, 2027 09:30–10:50, all room 109. Four slots do not allow four attempts: maximum three. Register at least 48 hours before, deregister at least 24 hours before. Printed bands: A 90.1–100, B 80.1–90, C 70.1–80, D 60.1–70, E 50.1–60, F 0–50; rounding and finer fractional gaps are unspecified. No personal deadline, chosen attempt or live SIS confirmation is established. Expanded Week 1 includes the introduction and completed social-preferences material; later syllabus topics/bibliographies are orientation. Misperceptions is conditional “if time allows” in SIS but unqualified in syllabus. Sample exam questions include inequality aversion, trust, ultimatum, statistical discrimination, discrimination-model comparisons and intergroup contact referring to Corno et al. (2022); the sample has no supplied answer key. Some social-preference concepts now have teaching notes, but discrimination/intergroup-contact readings and later-topic formal models/evidence remain outside this plan. Empty classroom polls are not results; this plan does not establish full-syllabus/exam readiness. The proposal conflicts with three-attempt and calculator restrictions and invents a supplied complete keyed syllabus.

**Checks and interpretation:** Verify labels, active conditions, dimensions/signs where applicable and every source limitation before inferring a choice. This example is exposed instruction and cannot establish independence.

