**Key:** The supplied documents identify Behavioral Economics JEB147, taught in person by Michal Bauer and Julie Chytilová, with Microeconomics I JEB104 as prerequisite. Preparation combines theory/intuition, experimental evidence and applications; slides are posted in SIS and papers are strongly encouraged. Bauer’s syllabus office is 402 with Friday 11:00–11:45; Chytilová’s is 410 with Friday 10:30–11:45. Email first; the introduction also says Bauer appointments can be arranged by email. These are supplied descriptions, not live availability. The grade is based on the written final: maximum 100 points, 80 minutes, one-correct-answer multiple choice and open-ended text, no negative points for incorrect answers. It is closed book: supporting materials, devices, calculators and AI tools prohibited; blank paper and pens allowed. Integrity-declaration agreement is required for evaluation; refusal is F. Listed exam slots are Dec 16, 2026 08:00–09:20; Jan 15, 2027 09:30–10:50; Jan 29, 2027 09:30–10:50; Feb 5, 2027 09:30–10:50, all room 109. Four slots do not allow four attempts: maximum three. Register at least 48 hours before, deregister at least 24 hours before. Printed bands: A 90.1–100, B 80.1–90, C 70.1–80, D 60.1–70, E 50.1–60, F 0–50; rounding and finer fractional gaps are unspecified. No personal deadline, chosen attempt or live SIS confirmation is established. Expanded Week 1 includes the introduction and completed social-preferences material; later syllabus topics/bibliographies are orientation. Misperceptions is conditional “if time allows” in SIS but unqualified in syllabus. Sample exam questions include inequality aversion, trust, ultimatum, statistical discrimination, discrimination-model comparisons and intergroup contact referring to Corno et al. (2022); the sample has no supplied answer key. Some social-preference concepts now have teaching notes, but discrimination/intergroup-contact readings and later-topic formal models/evidence remain outside this plan. Empty classroom polls are not results; this plan does not establish full-syllabus/exam readiness. Offered slots do not increase the three-attempt maximum or choose a personal deadline; the absent fractional/rounding rule must remain unspecified.

**Item rubric:** All listed required components must be supplied, with correct reasoning and limits; apply the component decision rules in Rubric and independence. Conceptually equivalent explanations and exact fractions/consistent decimals are acceptable. No assistance, notes, calculator, device, AI or key access for an independent/delayed attempt; blank paper and pen only. C1 is a supported repair after scoring the original response and does not count toward acquisition or delayed retention.

## Advance, revisit, pause, and escalate

Advance this objective only after committed qualifying passes on both I1 and I2; do not imply durable retention until an eligible delayed check is recorded. If a required diagnostic is deficient, follow its named prerequisite explanation/support. End a session with the actual unfinished route when acquisition is unmet. If relevant notes/state change or the matched journal is incomplete, block this version pending plan reconciliation. A source contradiction, missing academic explanation or absent provenance goes to ingest at the exact note/heading. Unsupported answer alternatives, ambiguous rubrics, missing prepared components or exhausted fresh I/D/C variants go to plan as `plan_issue`, with course ID, P001/V001/ingest 2, objective/criterion, item ID/version, exposed item IDs and the actual gap; do not invent or recycle a new assessment. Finite-bank exhaustion is a curriculum gap, not learner failure. No scheduler dates or learner status are written here.

